Green opportunities — prioritised

1. Quick win — instrument, de-duplicate and cache

Problem: Retries and unchanged inputs may repeat model work.
Change: Log every call per R; normalise the questionnaire; create an idempotency key; cache safe outputs; include prompt/model version in the cache key; regenerate only the affected section.
Pillars: Energy, hardware, measurement.
Patterns: Optimize agent orchestration to reduce unnecessary model calls and Cache static data.

2. Quick-to-medium — right-size models with a quality gate

Problem: One strong model may be used for tasks that rules or a smaller model can complete.
Change: Route validation and simple structure to deterministic logic/smaller models; reserve the stronger model for creative synthesis and difficult cases.
Pillars: Energy, hardware, measurement.
Pattern: Use right-sized and energy-efficient AI models.
Guardrail: A blinded evaluation must show non-inferior usefulness, specificity and brand distinctiveness.

3. Medium — stage media fidelity and automate retention

Problem: High-resolution drafts, unused variants and intermediate files may accumulate.
Change: Generate low-resolution drafts first; upscale only selected outputs; use modern image formats; set a documented deletion policy for rejected and intermediate media.
Pillars: Energy, hardware, measurement.
Patterns: Optimize image size, Serve images in modern formats and Set storage retention policies.

4. Medium-to-strategic — match infrastructure and timing to demand

Problem: An uneven MVP workload may leave app or automation capacity running while no user journey is active.
Change: Evaluate scale-to-zero/serverless execution; queue deferrable image work; review service region using data residency, latency, availability and grid carbon intensity together.
Pillars: Carbon, energy, hardware, measurement.
Patterns: Scale down applications when not in use and Use carbon-aware scheduling and region selection for AI workloads.

Measurement plan

Baseline period

Observe one representative week without changing the user journey. Record for every R:

model calls by type and model;

input/output tokens and image-generation settings;

retries, duplicate submissions and cacheable repetitions;

images generated, images selected and intermediate assets retained;

MB transferred and stored;

end-to-end latency, failure rate and API cost;

application/worker uptime, CPU and memory utilisation;

provider region and available grid-carbon-intensity information.

Retrofit period

Enable the four levers for a matched week or matched journey set. Keep the same R, boundary, prompts, evaluation rubric and comparison method.

Proposed success thresholds

at least 30% fewer model calls per R;

at least 25% fewer tokens per R;

at least 50% fewer intermediate bytes retained;

no material loss in usefulness, specificity or brand distinctiveness;

no unacceptable increase in latency, failure rate or user-visible cold starts.

These are decision thresholds, not current results.

Before/after hypotheses

If duplicate/retry work is a material share of current runs, idempotency and partial regeneration should reduce total model calls per R by approximately 20–30%.

If draft fidelity and retention are staged, intermediate image bytes should fall by approximately 40–70%.

If the hosting configuration safely supports scale-to-zero, application compute during no-traffic windows should approach zero, subject to the platform's underlying shared infrastructure.

The hypotheses must be replaced with measured results after the experiment.

Risks and trade-offs

A smaller model may reduce distinctiveness or accuracy; use routing and quality evaluation rather than a blind downgrade.

Caching can create stale results or retain sensitive inputs too long; version keys and retention controls are required.

Image compression and conversion consume CPU; the net effect must be tested against storage and transfer savings.

Scale-to-zero may introduce cold-start latency.

Region shifting may conflict with GDPR/data residency, increase network transfer, reduce service availability or worsen latency.

Provider energy, region and hardware allocation data may be incomplete, limiting a full SCI calculation.

Honest carbon framing

The audit does not claim that MIRA is carbon neutral. Tokens, calls, bytes, latency and cost are operational signals; cost is not a carbon metric. A defensible SCI-style result requires consistent energy E, grid carbon intensity I, allocated embodied hardware emissions M, boundary and functional unit R. Offsets do not reduce an SCI score.

